SUMMARY:[Department of Art & Art History / Stanford Arts Institute] Tangli
 ng the Digital: Weaving\, Printmaking\, Computing
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261027T170000
DTSTAMP:20261027T183442Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53948654465100-53948654496865@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Digital objects are often seen as alien to craft\, as though n
 o hands had shaped them. They can be copied endlessly and exactly\, bereft
  of the maker’s touch. Yet weaving and printmaking are among our oldest 
 technologies of reproduction\, fixing patterns so they can be repeated and
  circulated. The printing press and the Jacquard loom initiated processes 
 of automation that displaced human hands—and that still animate our digi
 tal tools.\n\nWarp and weft describe not just a textile structure but a lo
 gic\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-10-27 11:34 PDT
LOCATION:McMurtry Building
END:VEVENT
